entals": {"margin": 8.65, "lean": 4.5, "environment": 4.15, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 15.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 6.87, "baseline_margin": 12.46, "analyst_adjustment": 0.54, "key_factors": ["Colorado's D+6 lean and a Democratic-held governorship favor the open-seat Democratic nominee", "Republican nominee Victor Marx's controversial profile has split his own party, with independent Greg Lopez also on the ballot", "All seven expert raters place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets price Democrats at 96-97%", "No public general-election polling; fundraising is roughly even but Marx is spending his cash quickly"], "rationale": "An open-seat race in a state that leans six points Democratic, against a Republican nominee whose candidacy has fractured his own party, gives Weiser a clear edge. Race raters unanimously call it Solid or Safe Democratic and betting markets price Democratic chances around 96-97%, supporting a double-digit expected margin.", "watch": "With no polling available, a late survey showing Marx consolidating Republican voters while Lopez fades — or a major Weiser misstep — would be the clearest signal of a tighter race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Jared Polis (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Colorado's governor's race is an open-seat contest to succeed term-limited Democrat Jared Polis, pitting Attorney General Phil Weiser against Republican nominee Victor Marx, a candidate whose unconventional background has drawn national attention and divided his own party. Independent Greg Lopez and several minor candidates also appear on the ballot, giving voters alternatives beyond the two major parties.\n\nThe state's Democratic lean, Marx's difficulty unifying Republicans, and the presence of an independent drawing right-leaning votes all point toward the Democrat. No general